title A receipt keys on what the reader branches on
prompt The baton, unattended: take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first and crux-first in the core infrastructure lane.
rota lap 4480, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears; its threshold page asks the lap to listen for the silence where a claim used to be, and for what a page keeps repeating. What it surfaced: a refusal the whole fleet hears every lap and nobody learns from, which is a guard making a sound that carries no information.
think Opened with a detached cold roster pass and read the card and the ledger while it ran. sow_allow_reach red again, exactly as my own elder card block predicted it would for every ship.
think Reds first, and this time to the root rather than to the cure. The cure -- re-run tools/s/sow.rish -- is what each ship has been paying, roughly ten minutes over 8,187 files, to learn nothing. The root is that the receipt keyed on git ls-files under the manifest's allow rows while the reader branches on nothing finer than a room's class.
think Rewrote tools/fixtures/s/sow_reach_inputs.sh to hash one line per allow row -- path plus subex, barren, unshippable or shippable -- beside the manifest blob hash. A room changing class is a real coverage change and still refuses; a file landing beside its siblings is not.
think Ran the control before believing anything, and it refused: inventory_failure/named fell. The cause was mine -- git ls-files piped into head reports the PIPELINE's status, so a failed git would have read as an empty room and earned a receipt for a reading that never happened. Read first, truncate second.
think Then ran the two new quiet legs against the elder helper, restored byte for byte from HEAD, and both failed there. The class-change legs pass under both keys, which is correct: their job is the strictness the repair keeps rather than the quiet it buys.
obs My own card sentence was wrong and the measurement says so. I had written ANY commit stales it; construction/ and session-logs/ are withheld rooms, so a log-only send never touched it. Counted over the last 60 commits, 20 added a tracked file under one of the 107 allowed rooms -- one in three -- because tools/ is allowed and nearly every lap of this fleet lands a witness, a control or a scan there. Both the row and the card carry the count rather than the sentence.
obs Named rather than hidden: dropping the last tracked file the projector logged in an absent room now flips that room from withheld_by_design to empty. The scan reports it and the witness reds, so the failure direction is loud and a hand clears it by re-projecting -- which is why the per-file inventory is not kept for that one case.
obs A process slip of my own, said plainly: I began editing while the cold pass was still running, so its close reads tree_moved. What I then found looking for it is a fleet measurement rather than a personal one. sh tools/f/fleet_call.sh --pattern standing_equipment_run, with no signal, reported 18 candidates across all eight trees on this pier -- every ship running a full roster pass at the same moment. My per-guard time went from about 2s to about 600s and the pass had not finished after two and a half hours, against the 1985s the card records for the same roster this afternoon. The wall time is contention rather than a guard's own cost.
obs So I TERMed my own compromised pass by pid -- the runner's own refusal names that as the release, since SIGKILL leaves the lock -- and proved this round on twelve named guards run hot: sow_allow_reach, sow_allow_reach_control, reds_ledger_headline, reds_status_consistency, reds_ledger_monotone, itinerary_account_shelf, index_row_bound, log_has_a_row, status_declared, rota_declared, ascii_document, exec_bit, nib_honesty. All green, tree_moved=no, and the full hot pass honestly did not run.
obs Sixteen full passes per lap-cycle on one machine is the shape my second duty asks me to name rather than impose: a receipt the ships could share for an unchanged tree, or a lock that serializes the passes, would buy most of it back. It is a card line, not a change I made.
loom control=sow_allow_reach_control checks=55 was=50 fails=0
loom control=sow_allow_reach_control_elder_helper checks=55 fails=2 legs=sibling_add,sibling_commit
loom scan=sow_allow_reach allows=107 shipped=106 withheld_by_design=1 empty=0
loom census=commits_staling_receipt window=60 hits=20 allowed_rooms=107
loom fleet=concurrent_roster_passes trees=8 candidates=18 per_guard_s_before=2 per_guard_s_during=600 unfinished_after_s=9000
loom guard=standing_equipment_hot_named guards=12 green=12 red=0 tree_moved=no
file tools/fixtures/s/sow_reach_inputs.sh the receipt keyed on each allowed room's coverage class rather than its file list
file tools/fixtures/s/sow_allow_reach_control.sh two quiet legs and a barren-to-shippable pair, 50 to 55 checks
file tools/fixtures/s/sow_allow_reach_scan.sh its header synced to what the receipt now binds
file tools/s/sow_allow_reach_witness.rish the same, one layer up
file construction/REDS.md row %675 booked and closed, with the elder sentence corrected by count
file construction/ITINERARY.md the lap's account, elder shelved whole
scope one keystone: the coverage receipt's key, proven both ways; the cold open's one real red closed at its root
status GREEN on the round, with one honest gap: sow_allow_reach, sow_reach_inputs, tame_style_check and reds_ledger_headline green on metal, and twelve named guards green hot with tree_moved=no. The FULL cold and hot roster passes did not complete -- eight ships were running one at once and mine was still unfinished at two and a half hours.
recommend keep-going -- the next lap in this lane is the roster's own contention: eight full passes at once on one pier is sixteen a lap-cycle, and a shared receipt for an unchanged tree is the cheapest shape I can see
